%% SECTION Foundations, Value Delivery and Principles
@@
Which characteristic distinguishes a project from operations?
A) Projects are temporary and produce unique results; operations are ongoing and repetitive
B) Projects always have larger budgets and more senior sponsors than any ongoing operations
C) Operations have formal stakeholders, while projects only have team members
D) Projects maintain existing capabilities, while operations create new ones
= A
? Projects are temporary endeavours that create unique products, services or results; operations sustain the business through repetitive work. Projects usually change operations.

@@
A city launches several related projects — new bus lanes, a ticketing app and a park-and-ride facility — coordinated to reduce traffic congestion. How is this collection best described?
A) A program
B) A portfolio
C) A single project with three phases
D) Operations
= A
? Related projects coordinated to achieve benefits (reduced congestion) that would not be achieved by managing them separately form a program.

@@
Which statement best describes the relationship between outputs, outcomes and benefits?
A) Outputs are what is produced; outcomes result from using them; benefits are the gains that follow
B) Benefits are produced first, then converted into outputs that create the outcomes stakeholders see
C) Outcomes are the deliverables, outputs are the gains, and benefits are the activities producing them
D) The three terms are interchangeable labels for the deliverables accepted at project closure
= A
? Activities create outputs; using the outputs creates outcomes; outcomes produce benefits; together they contribute to value.

@@
In the system for value delivery, what is the primary purpose of portfolio management?
A) Selecting and prioritising investments to achieve strategic objectives
B) Coordinating the detailed schedules of related projects to share resources efficiently
C) Running day-to-day operations so that project deliverables are sustained
D) Approving each project's change requests to keep baselines under control
= A
? Portfolios align investments with strategy, balance risk and allocate resources. Detailed scheduling and change approval occur at project level.

@@
Which of the following is an internal enterprise environmental factor?
A) The organisation's geographic distribution of facilities and staff
B) Industry standards published by a professional association for its members
C) Interest rates set by the central bank
D) Weather conditions at the site
= A
? Geographic distribution of facilities is internal to the organisation and not under the team's immediate control. Industry standards, interest rates and weather are external.

@@
A project manager uses templates and historical estimates from a company database to plan a new project. These are best classified as:
A) Organisational process assets
B) Enterprise environmental factors
C) Project deliverables
D) Work performance data
= A
? Templates, historical information and knowledge repositories belong to the performing organisation and are OPAs.

@@
In a strong matrix organisation, which statement is typically true?
A) The project manager has moderate to high authority and is assigned full-time
B) The functional manager controls the budget and the project manager coordinates part-time
C) The project manager acts as an expediter without authority over resources or budget
D) Team members report only to the project manager and have no functional manager
= A
? In a strong matrix, PM authority and resource availability are moderate to high, and the PM is full-time. Team members still have functional managers.

@@
Who usually issues the project charter?
A) The sponsor or project initiator
B) The project manager
C) The project team
D) The procurement department, on behalf of the PMO
= A
? The charter is issued by someone external to the project at a level able to fund and authorise it — usually the sponsor.

@@ | multi
Which two items are commonly found in a business case? (Choose two.)
A) Analysis of the business need and recommended option
B) Cost-benefit analysis with financial measures such as NPV
C) Detailed activity durations
D) The team's ground rules
E) Daily stand-up notes
= A, B
? A business case justifies the investment: it describes the need, analyses options, recommends one and includes cost-benefit analysis. Activity durations and team artifacts belong elsewhere.

@@
A team member asks why the project bothers to measure benefits after go-live, since the project will have closed. What is the best explanation?
A) Many benefits appear only after delivery, so measuring them confirms the value created
B) Benefit tracking after closure is mainly needed to calculate the team's performance bonuses
C) Measuring benefits after go-live is required only when the project used an agile approach
D) It is not needed, because success is fully determined by scope, time and cost at closure
= A
? Value is often realised after the project closes. The benefit owner tracks benefits to confirm the business case and inform future decisions.

@@
Which principle is most directly applied when a project manager considers how a design decision will affect maintenance costs and energy use over the product's 20-year life?
A) Integrate sustainability within all project areas
B) Build an empowered culture
C) Be an accountable leader
D) Embed quality, because energy use is a defect in the delivered product
= A
? Considering long-term environmental and economic impacts over the product life cycle reflects sustainability, while also reflecting a holistic view.

@@
A project manager notices that optimising the project's schedule would overload a shared testing team needed by two other critical projects. Which principle should guide the response?
A) Adopt a holistic view
B) Focus on value only for this project
C) Build an empowered culture
D) Embed quality into deliverables
= A
? A holistic view considers the project as part of a larger system, including shared resources and other initiatives.

@@
Which behaviour best demonstrates the principle "Build an empowered culture"?
A) Inviting people to raise concerns and letting the team decide within agreed boundaries
B) Requiring the project manager's approval for decisions so that accountability stays clear
C) Publicly naming people who make mistakes so that the whole team learns from them
D) Limiting stakeholder involvement to formal monthly reports to keep the team focused
= A
? Empowered cultures rely on psychological safety, trust and delegated decision-making with clear boundaries.

@@
A senior developer proposes adding extra features that no one has requested, arguing they will "add quality". Which principle helps the project manager respond?
A) Embed quality — quality means meeting requirements and fitness for use, not extra features
B) Focus on value — any additional feature increases the value delivered to the customer
C) Adopt a holistic view — a larger scope improves the system as a whole
D) Be an accountable leader — the senior developer should decide on all technical scope alone
= A
? Gold plating is not quality. Quality is defined by requirements and real stakeholder needs; extra features add cost and risk without agreed value.

@@
How many principles does the Eighth Edition define, and how many did the Seventh Edition define?
A) Six in the Eighth Edition; twelve in the Seventh Edition
B) Twelve in the Eighth Edition; six in the Seventh Edition
C) Eight in both editions
D) Seven in the Eighth Edition; ten in the Seventh Edition
= A
? The Eighth Edition consolidated the Seventh Edition's twelve principles into six.

@@
Which side of the PMI Talent Triangle covers understanding the organisation's strategy, finances and market?
A) Business Acumen
B) Ways of Working
C) Power Skills
D) Technical Leadership
= A
? Business Acumen connects projects to strategy and value through knowledge of the business, finance, markets and regulations.

@@
What does it mean that the PMBOK Guide is "non-prescriptive"?
A) It describes good practice that teams adapt to their context instead of mandating procedures
B) It prescribes standard templates that every organisation must adopt without changes
C) It is a theoretical reference that is not intended to be used on real projects
D) It applies only to predictive projects, while agile projects follow the Agile Practice Guide
= A
? The Guide describes what is generally good practice; the team tailors processes, methods and artifacts to the context.

@@
A government agency must deliver a new tax portal because of a new law. Which category of project driver does this represent?
A) Regulatory, legal or social requirement
B) Stakeholder request or need
C) Business or technological strategy
D) Creating, improving or fixing a product
= A
? Projects initiated to comply with new laws or regulations fall under regulatory, legal or social requirements.

@@
Which role is usually responsible for prioritising investments across many projects when resources are scarce?
A) The portfolio manager or portfolio governance board
B) Each project manager, negotiating independently with the sponsors
C) The scrum master of the largest team
D) The PMO's quality assurance analysts
= A
? Resource conflicts across projects that affect strategy are resolved at portfolio level, which prioritises investments.

@@ | match
Match each term with its description.
1) Program
2) Portfolio
3) Operations
4) Product
A) Related components coordinated to obtain benefits
B) Components managed as a group to achieve strategic objectives
C) Ongoing work that sustains the organisation
D) An artifact that may be created or changed by several projects over its life cycle
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Programs coordinate for benefits; portfolios align investments to strategy; operations sustain the business; products persist over a life cycle that can span many projects.

@@
A project manager discovers that a vendor's materials contain a substance that violates environmental regulations, although it is cheaper. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop using the materials, inform the right stakeholders and source a compliant alternative
B) Use the materials, since the savings were approved in the budget and the client wants low cost
C) Continue with the materials but record the issue in the risk register for later review
D) Ask the vendor to provide documentation that classifies the substance as acceptable
= A
? Accountable leadership, sustainability and compliance require transparency and adherence to laws. Cost savings never justify illegal or unethical actions.

@@
Which document describes target benefits, the timeframe for realising them and who is accountable for them?
A) Benefits management plan
B) Project scope statement
C) Communications management plan
D) Issue log
= A
? The benefits management plan defines benefits, metrics, timeframe, benefit owner, assumptions and risks.

@@
What is the main reason projects should report honestly upward through the value delivery system?
A) Leadership needs accurate information to adjust strategy and investments
B) Honest reports are required mainly to pass audits at the end of the project
C) Status reports determine the project manager's annual performance rating
D) Operations teams use project reports to approve the project budget each month
= A
? The value delivery system depends on feedback loops; inaccurate status prevents good portfolio and strategic decisions.

@@
A project manager is told to "act as an integrator". What does this mean?
A) Bringing plans, work, people and changes together into a coherent whole
B) Personally performing the technical integration of all system components
C) Approving every change request quickly so that the plan stays integrated
D) Delegating the alignment of plans to the sponsor and functional managers
= A
? Integration is the PM's core role: aligning processes, knowledge, context and complexity to deliver outcomes.

@@
Which statement about value is correct according to the Standard?
A) Value can be tangible or intangible and may be realised during or after the project
B) Value is measured only by profit and is confirmed when the final invoice is paid
C) Value is realised at project closure, when all deliverables are formally accepted by the customer
D) Value equals the approved budget, because it reflects what stakeholders will pay
= A
? Value includes financial and non-financial benefits such as reputation or public good, and is often realised progressively or after closure.
